docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md:44:- **Salsa** (rust-analyzer's incremental-computation engine) as monokl's own memoized query cache, decoupled from any `ra_ap_hir` decision. Real standalone precedent exists (Astral's `ty` Python type checker, Biome's analyzer layer both use Salsa independent of rust-analyzer's own queries) — monokl could define its own queries (symbols-for-file, postings-for-term) memoized on content hash without ever loading a crate graph, a materially lighter proposition than embedding `ra_ap_hir` wholesale. Worth a scoped spike, tracked separately from the item below.
docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md:50:- **Embedding-based semantic rerank by default.** No code-specific evidence shows it beats lexical+structural signals for single-codebase retrieval (identifiers matter more than paraphrase within one codebase), and every tool surveyed that uses it depends on server-side vector-DB infrastructure — none of the local-first CLI tools examined run it by default. If ever added, gate behind an explicit opt-in flag; it is not this spec's default posture. The stronger version of this argument is a decade of established code-intelligence practice — CodeQL and LSIF/SCIP have relied on exact, compiler/AST-derived graphs rather than embeddings for precise code intelligence long before RAG was a term. A small, weakly-evidenced preprint (Chinthareddy, "Reliable Graph-RAG for Codebases," arXiv:2601.08773 — single-author, no institutional affiliation in this field, unreviewed, n=45 questions across 3 repos, no independent replication; vetted and found directionally-consistent-but-not-rigorous) reports numbers in the same direction (a deterministic AST graph answering 43/45 benchmark questions vs. 31/45 for vector-only similarity, and LLM-generated dependency graphs missing 377 of 1210 files at indexing time while costing 70-150× more to build than deterministic extraction) — cite it only as an early, consistent data point, not as load-bearing proof. Its own described "bidirectional traversal" is, per the paper's actual source, a single 1-hop successor/predecessor expansion rather than genuine multi-hop reasoning — worth knowing before assuming it validates more traversal depth than it actually tested. Independent evidence either way against ever LLM-generating the workspace graph as an alternative to parsing it deterministically — a design path this spec never considered but is now explicitly closed off, on the stronger CodeQL/SCIP grounding rather than the preprint alone.
docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md:138:- **C** — clean grammar (`tree-sitter/tree-sitter-c`), mature, widely embedded elsewhere (difftastic, ast-grep, nvim-treesitter), open issues cluster on C23/C2y feature gaps rather than fundamental ambiguity. TIOBE #2.
docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md:183:**Kotlin and Scala: an optional JVM-subprocess compiler-parse tier, distinct from the daemon question.** Both languages' real "compiler as a library" story turned out more nuanced than a flat "daemon-only." For Kotlin: `kotlin-compiler-embeddable` (what `detekt`/`ktlint` historically used) is being abandoned — it's incompatible with the newer Analysis API, confirmed by detekt's own migration issue (detekt#8027, closed May 2026). The hoped-for lighter K2 frontend for external tools does not exist yet either — JetBrains' own docs state standalone mode "is currently in development and subject to incompatible changes," with IntelliJ plugin development the only officially supported use case today. But a real, production-proven middle ground already exists independent of both of those: `ktlint` and `detekt`'s **default** (non-type-resolution) mode runs directly on the compiler's real PSI tree and needs no classpath at all — "parsing a Kotlin file does not require any knowledge about the project it belongs to," per their own docs. That's real compiler-grade parsing, clearing well past tree-sitter's 61.2% ceiling, at the cost of a JVM subprocess rather than an in-process library call. `detekt`'s further opt-in type-resolution tier (needs `--classpath`) is the genuine daemon-territory tier, same shape as Java's `jdtls` — that part of the original assumption holds unchanged.
docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md:195:Headline result, in brief: the core shape survived (every backend maps onto `PerCall`/ `PerInvocation`/`Session` as a real cost ceiling, no language demanded a fourth tier), but the trait's _return type_ did not — five independent per-language studies (Kotlin, Go, Scala, Rust, C++) converged on the same finding that "promote a bare `CapabilityPrecision`" destroys real candidate sets, dual precision axes, and structured facts the underlying tools actually hand back. Also produced: real backend identification per language (`typescript-go`/TS 7.0's new LSP server went GA July 8, 2026, superseding the plain-tsserver assumption; Python's `Pyrefly`/`ty` are genuinely Rust-implemented but still subprocess/LSP-integrated, not embeddable libraries; Java's `jdtls` import step hard-crashes via OOM past ~3000 files, a real gate requirement, not a graceful degradation; Kotlin's `kotlin-lsp` is real but still built on the same unstable-per-JetBrains foundation already flagged, just hidden behind a heavier IDE-embedding shell); and a confirmed, structural gap in both LSP and SCIP — neither protocol carries a confidence field, so the compilers' own internal degradation signals (Clang's `RecoveryExpr`, JDT's `isRecovered()`) have to be recovered via a per-backend sentinel table, not trusted from protocol silence.
