23:- **Build an eval harness before further ranking/tuning decisions are made on faith.** BM25's `K1=1.5`/`B=0.5` and the token-budget defaults are carried over from Probe's prior tuning (§29) and are unvalidated for monokl specifically. Minimum viable harness: two layers, not one — (a) a deterministic retrieval eval (RepoBench-R- or CodeSearchNet-style recall@k/MRR) run on every commit, and (b) a properly-powered agent A/B (model/effort pinned and logged, ≥5 runs per condition reporting median + IQR, ≥10 repos with a task list frozen before either arm runs). Report token and dollar-cost deltas _separately_, broken out by cache-read vs. fresh-input token mix — codegraph's own published cost-savings claim reversed under independent replication specifically because token/call reductions and dollar cost decoupled when its context payload shifted from many cheap cached reads to fewer expensive fresh reads. Cheapest first step: fill in the four already-stubbed `benches/pipeline.rs` criterion groups (`pipeline_cold`, `pipeline_warm_cache`, `dependents_warm`, `lite_symbols`, §29) — every cold/warm latency target asserted elsewhere in this spec is currently unmeasured, including in monokl's own benchmark suite.
25:- **Add a `TraitImplEnricher` / `TraitImplIndex`** for Rust — a reverse index (`FxHashMap<String, Vec<(FileIdx, usize)>>` keyed by trait name, pointing at implementor sites) inverting the per-struct `RustStructEntry.trait_impls: Vec<String>` across the whole corpus. This is the structural signal that upstream discovery (interface/trait → implementors — controllers, services, handlers wired through a trait) actually depends on, and monokl's current design has no such index anywhere. Follows the exact `SymbolIndexEnricher` shape already in this spec: `update()` pushes one `(FileIdx, decl_offset)` per trait per file; `finalize()` sorts each bucket by `(FileIdx, offset)` for determinism. `petgraph` was considered and rejected for this and for `ImportGraph` generally — its `NodeIndex` would either leak as the public handle (undermining `FileIdx`'s deliberate non-`Serialize` guard) or need wrapping anyway for no gain, and it pulls in dependencies (`fixedbitset`, `hashbrown`, `indexmap`) for graph algorithms (SCC, Dijkstra) monokl doesn't use; the existing hand-rolled `FxHashMap` adjacency-list approach already matches what rust-analyzer's own `trait_impls_in_crate_query` and rustdoc's implementors-list generation do structurally (a dedicated reverse index built during a full pass), differing only by the Salsa incremental caching this spec deliberately forgoes. "Upstream discovery" for a trait `T` then composes three existing/new indices — no fourth graph structure needed — via a capped BFS: `TraitImplIndex` → implementor sites, cross-referenced against `SymbolIndex` for usage sites, expanded through `ImportGraph` reverse edges. That BFS should adopt the same hardening codegraph's own `GraphTraverser` needed in production: an `FxHashSet<FileIdx>` visited-set checked at enqueue time (not just dequeue), an explicit depth limit, and a result cap checked at every push site rather than only the loop head. Surface a cap-hit as a first-class, matchable outcome — extend `MonoklError` with a non-fatal `TraversalCapExceeded { visited: usize, cap: usize }` variant, returned inside a `TraversalReport { sites: Vec<_>, truncated: bool }` rather than silently truncating a bare `Vec`. The same bounded-BFS pattern generalizes to any of this spec's existing traversals (`dependents`/`imports_of`, impact-radius-style queries) that don't yet have explicit cap enforcement at every site.
32:- **Budget tree-sitter-backed languages (current and any future fallback-tier additions) at their cold-parse cost, with no incremental-reparse credit** (full research in §11). Monokl never holds a previous tree to diff against — every invocation is a fresh process reading from cache, not a live-edit session — so tree-sitter's headline incremental-reparse advantage doesn't apply to monokl's actual usage pattern. Do not port any externally-published parser benchmark number (OXC's own, tree-sitter's informal maintainer figures, codegraph's ~60 files/sec) into `benches/pipeline.rs` as a target threshold — none are methodology-comparable to monokl's own file mix. The stubbed `pipeline_cold` benchmark (§2 above) is the only trustworthy source of a real number here; until it runs, treat this spec's existing ~15s cold-build target as unvalidated and plausibly optimistic, not as a number to relax.
34:- **An optional JVM-subprocess `Structural`-tier analyzer for Kotlin (compiler PSI) and Scala (Scalameta)** (full research in §12) — both are compiler-grade parse-only modes that need no classpath, clearing well past tree-sitter's ceiling for each language (Kotlin's grammar tops out at 61.2% structural accuracy; Scala's has no Scala 3 support at all). Real production precedent for both (ktlint/detekt's default mode; Scalafmt/Scalafix; and for Scala specifically, a direct precedent for monokl's own use case, `scalex` — "Scala code intelligence for coding agents. Zero Build Server. Zero Compilation."). True semantic resolution for either language still needs a real classpath and stays on the same deferred daemon/session-mode track as `ra_ap_hir` (§4) — this tier is a new middle rung, not a shortcut past that.
44:- **Salsa** (rust-analyzer's incremental-computation engine) as monokl's own memoized query cache, decoupled from any `ra_ap_hir` decision. Real standalone precedent exists (Astral's `ty` Python type checker, Biome's analyzer layer both use Salsa independent of rust-analyzer's own queries) — monokl could define its own queries (symbols-for-file, postings-for-term) memoized on content hash without ever loading a crate graph, a materially lighter proposition than embedding `ra_ap_hir` wholesale. Worth a scoped spike, tracked separately from the item below.
45:- **`ra_ap_hir` integration and any persistent daemon/session mode are a coupled pair.** `ra_ap_hir`'s `Semantics` gives real Rust name resolution and type inference, but needs a populated `HirDatabase` via `cargo metadata` + crate-graph construction — a cost pegged by rust-analyzer's own maintainers at roughly 5 seconds per 100 crates just waiting on rustc. That cost model fits a warm, long-lived process, not a cold per-invocation CLI. Symmetrically, a persistent index/daemon mode (mirroring codegraph's watcher architecture) only earns its own cost past roughly 50k files — below that, the current stateless design's per-invocation fold cost (re-aggregating already-cached per-file analyses into `ImportGraph`/`SymbolIndex`, not re-parsing) is estimated at 100-500ms at 10k files and 300ms-1.5s at 50k, a small fraction of the 15s cold-build budget. Revisit both together, after the eval harness (§2) and the stubbed benchmarks (§2) produce real numbers — not before.
60:**`lang-go`** — **`gosyn`** ([chikaku/gosyn](https://github.com/chikaku/gosyn)) is a real, viable native Rust parser: 152k+ downloads, weekly commits, MIT-licensed, confirmed Go 1.18+ generics support (`parse_type_parameters`, generic instantiation/pointer types both present in source and tests). This is the strongest native-parser candidate found across every language evaluated this session — unlike Python and Java, Go did not need to fall back to tree-sitter. `go/packages.Load` (real semantic analysis) is daemon-only by the same argument as `ra_ap_hir`: a 20k-package monorepo took 3m26s cold to load ([golang/go#69523](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/69523)), and even a warm gopls daemon took 10+ seconds to serve a new client on a 2,047-package repo ([golang/go#48829](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/48829)). Import resolution is genuinely easier than Rust's for the common case — no macros, no monomorphization, and a `go.mod` module directive plus import-path-prefix-stripping gives a project-relative directory directly (mirror `resolve_rust_path`'s shape, but simpler). Two gaps explicitly out of scope for v1: `go.work` multi-module workspaces (unhandled — root `go.mod` only), and `//go:build`-tag-gated files (gopls itself cannot auto-detect arbitrary build tags beyond the `_linux.go`-style filename convention; a structural tool will see phantom duplicate symbols across tag-gated variants of the same declaration). `CapabilityPrecision::Structural`. Tokenizer: extend `STOP_WORDS` with `func`, `package`, `chan`, `defer`, `select`, `range`, `go`, `iota` when `lang-go` lands (`new`/`super`/`interface`/`struct` are already covered by existing entries).
74:**ast-grep** ([ast-grep/ast-grep](https://github.com/ast-grep/ast-grep)), read end-to-end (core matcher, rule DSL, language registration, CLI worker — not just the README). A pattern is parsed by the target language's own tree-sitter grammar into a `PatternNode` tree (`convert_node_to_pattern`, `crates/core/src/matcher/pattern.rs`), then matched via a generic `Aggregator` trait implemented once for the real match environment and once for a cheap match-end-only counter — one recursive algorithm serving both "does it match" and "where does it end." The YAML rule DSL has three tiers (atomic: `pattern`/`kind`/`regex`/`nthChild`/`range`; relational: `inside`/`has`/`precedes`/`follows` with a `stopBy` and field-targeted matching via `child_by_field_id`; composite: `all`/`any`/`not`/named reusable `matches` rules with cycle detection). A `MatchStrictness` enum (`Cst/Smart/Ast/Relaxed/Signature/Template`, `crates/core/src/match_tree/strictness.rs`) tunes how literally a pattern must match, down to "text only, ignore node kind." Adding a language is a one-line macro pointed at a tree-sitter crate, with no node-kind normalization layer — rules are not portable across languages, and grammar bugs leak directly into matcher correctness (a documented tree-sitter-typescript span bug is explicitly worked around in the strictness module). Ranking and caching are both genuinely absent — grepped the whole repo for anything score/rank/relevance-shaped and found nothing; matches return in file order, and every invocation fully reparses every file with no persistent cache of any kind (mtime, content-hash, or otherwise), using `ignore::WalkParallel` rather than rayon. This is not a gap relative to monokl — ast-grep doesn't attempt ranked/semantic search at all, so BM25, dedup, and token budgeting have no ast-grep prior art to compare against, favorable or not. See §2 for the `MatchStrictness`/relational-rule adoption.
82:**Class Hierarchy Analysis (CHA)** — Dean, Grove, Chambers, "Optimization of Object-Oriented Programs Using Static Class Hierarchy Analysis," ECOOP 1995, pp. 77-101. CHA resolves a virtual call from the declared class/interface hierarchy alone: any subtype of the static receiver type is a possible target, full stop — no dataflow, no reachability, no instantiation check. Fast, purely syntactic, known-unsound (ignores reflection, dynamic loading, generics-driven dispatch). This is `TraitImplIndex`'s (§2) shape exactly: it indexes every `impl Trait for Type` from the AST with no check on whether `Type` is ever constructed — a declared-hierarchy fact, not a program-behavior fact. `Structural` is a correct label for this.
84:**Rapid Type Analysis (RTA)** — Bacon & Sweeney, "Fast Static Analysis of C++ Virtual Function Calls," OOPSLA 1996, pp. 324-341. RTA refines CHA with one extra static pass: track which classes are actually instantiated (`new`) anywhere in the reachable program and prune CHA's target set to those. On their benchmarks (7 C++ programs, 5-20 KLOC) this resolved 71% of virtual calls statically, vs. CHA's much larger over-approximation. Critically, RTA is still single-pass, non-iterative, and needs no aliasing or points-to information — it costs the same order as CHA, not the order of a real type checker. That means RTA is not a stand-in for `Exact`; it is a finer-grained rung _inside_ what monokl currently collapses into one `Structural` tier. Concretely: CHA-shaped = "declared hierarchy says `Type` could implement `Trait`"; RTA-shaped = "...and `Type` is actually constructed somewhere in this corpus." An RTA-style filter over `TraitImplIndex` (§2) — cross-referencing implementor types against observed struct-literal/constructor sites, already information monokl's `SymbolIndex` pass collects — is a legitimate, still-`Structural`-cost enhancement worth recording, not a new precision tier.
86:**k-CFA** — Shivers, "Control-Flow Analysis of Higher-Order Languages, or Taming Lambda," PhD thesis, CMU-CS-91-145, May 1991 (formalizing the earlier "Control-Flow Analysis in Scheme," PLDI 1988, pp. 164-174). k-CFA answers a different question than CHA/RTA: not "which subtype could this be" but "which closure could this call site invoke," by abstracting environments to k-deep call-string contexts. It is EXPTIME-hard for any static k>0 — Van Horn & Mairson, "Deciding kCFA is complete for EXPTIME," ICFP 2008, pp. 275-282 — and the blow-up is fundamental, not an implementation artifact (companion result: Might, Smaragdakis, Van Horn, "Resolving and Exploiting the k-CFA Paradox," PLDI 2010, showing even k=1 degenerates badly on OO-flavored code specifically because class-based dispatch, monokl's actual target, isn't what k-CFA's context-sensitivity is built to disambiguate). k-CFA is the wrong shape and the wrong cost class for monokl: it targets first-class-closure flow in dynamically-typed/functional code, not "what implements this interface" or "what does this import resolve to." Tellingly, even IDE-scale JS tooling built for monokl's actual problem class explicitly avoids it — Feldthaus, Schäfer, Sridharan, Dolby, Tip, "Efficient Construction of Approximate Call Graphs for JavaScript IDE Services," ICSE 2013, use a cheaper field-based approximate analysis instead of full k-CFA specifically to hit interactive latency. Verdict: k-CFA is correctly out of scope (§5); no part of this spec should be revised toward it.
88:**Points-to analysis** — the two foundational styles: Andersen, "Program Analysis and Specialization for the C Programming Language," PhD thesis, DIKU, University of Copenhagen, 1994 (inclusion-based/subset-constraint, more precise, worse complexity); Steensgaard, "Points-to Analysis in Almost Linear Time," POPL 1996, pp. 32-41 (equality-based/union-find, "almost linear," less precise). Bravenboer & Smaragdakis, "Strictly Declarative Specification of Sophisticated Points-to Analyses," OOPSLA 2009 (the Doop framework), show Andersen-style points-to specified in Datalog scales across a dial of context-sensitivity (context-insensitive up to k-object-sensitive) rather than being one fixed cost point — the literature's actual answer for what a non-`ra_ap_hir` path toward `Exact` looks like, relevant to the open Salsa/persistent-daemon track (§4) as a second candidate design, not just `ra_ap_hir`.
112:- **[kubernetes/kubernetes](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes)** — 123,894 stars, Apache-2.0, 1.5GB / 30,977 files, active. Large real-world production distributed-systems Go at scale — the size ceiling case for the corpus.
123:- **[eugenp/tutorials](https://github.com/eugenp/tutorials)** — replaces the originally-proposed "Spring Boot + Lombok app," which turned out not to be a real, checkable single repo. This one is: 37,332 stars, MIT, 132MB / 26,277+ files (truncated, true total higher), active, and Lombok usage is **confirmed** directly (`lombok.version` pinned in multiple module POMs, e.g. `persistence-modules/pom.xml`) alongside heavy Spring Boot annotation use across hundreds of real modules. Tradeoff worth naming: it's a grab-bag of independent tutorial modules, not one cohesive application — good for idiom breadth, not for whole-program call-graph-style checks.
150:**Graduation path back to a dedicated analyzer.** A fallback-tier language earns Go/Java-style bespoke investment when it clears both: (a) sustained usage in the current Go/Java tier (roughly top 10-12 on TIOBE/Octoverse) _and_ (b) documented evidence of a structural-precision ceiling analogous to Java's Lombok problem — idioms common enough in real code that `Heuristic`/ `Structural`-only output silently breaks call-chain or symbol resolution for a large fraction of real codebases. **C++ already clears both today**, and §12 answers what that graduation actually looks like: not a from-scratch native-Rust parser (mirroring OXC/`gosyn`/`ra_ap_syntax` turned out to be the wrong shape here — C++'s parsing ambiguity is a real semantic-analysis problem, not a grammar-engineering one no pure-Rust parser could plausibly solve better than tree-sitter already does), but an optional Clang-gated analyzer. See §12 for the full recommendation and for Kotlin and Scala, which have their own version of this same "held back from the tree-sitter tier, but not stuck there" story.
156:**What's real and citable**: OXC publishes its own parse-only benchmarks (`oxc-project/bench-javascript-parser-written-in-rust`, `iter_with_large_drop` to exclude AST- teardown cost) — concretely, `cal.com.tsx` parses in 3.4ms vs. 14.4ms for swc and 18.3ms for Biome (the Biome comparison is explicitly flagged non-apples-to-apples, CST vs. AST); `typescript.js` parses in 26.4ms vs. 91.0ms (swc) and 139.3ms (Biome). **Tree-sitter is not in OXC's own comparison set at all** — there is no published OXC-vs-tree-sitter-typescript number anywhere.
158:**What tree-sitter itself publishes is thin.** No official throughput figures exist. The closest data points: an informal maintainer comment (tree-sitter/tree-sitter Discussion #3413) citing a 6k-line file parsed in ~80ms by a custom grammar and a 20k-line file parsed in ~120ms by the Python grammar, presented as "comparable," not a rigorous benchmark; and, more usefully, tree-sitter/ tree-sitter issue #1277, which reports a 1.6MB JSON file taking ~1.2s for a full cold parse and **~0.7s to re-parse with zero edits**, using ~300MB for two trees. That no-op-reparse number is direct evidence that tree-sitter's incremental-parse advantage has a real floor even in its best case — it is not free.
162:**Real gaps, stated plainly rather than papered over.** No published head-to-head benchmark exists for `ra_ap_syntax` vs. `tree-sitter-rust`, a native Go parser vs. `tree-sitter-go`, or any Python native-parser candidate vs. `tree-sitter-python` (Ruff's own parser-evolution writeup benchmarks only against its own predecessor, never against tree-sitter). `gosyn` publishes zero benchmark numbers. Among comparable tools already researched in this log: ast-grep's own "10x faster" writeup attributes its win entirely to regex-cloning and `potential_kinds`-matching fixes, saying nothing about parse-cost scaling; difftastic's "4x faster" writeup confirms its bottleneck was `Vertex`/diff-graph memory layout, not parsing, but never publishes a parse-time-vs-diff-time split; codegraph reports ~60 files/sec end-to-end (parse+extract+write combined, no per-language breakdown) — extrapolated naively, that's ~167s for 10k files, which should make this spec's own existing ~15s cold-build target read as unvalidated and plausibly optimistic, not as a number any of this research should be used to relax.
168:- Memory is a real open question, not a closed one: the one available data point (a 1.6MB JSON file consuming ~300MB across two trees) suggests tree-sitter's transient peak during parsing can be nontrivial, and no fleet-scale (10k-50k file) comparison against native parsers exists publicly. Given this spec's own arena-containment rule (no borrowed AST data may escape a per-file `analyze()` call — every `FileAnalysis` must be owned), the actual risk is the transient peak during conversion to owned data, not steady-state footprint — worth a targeted monokl-internal check once real code exists, not something to resolve from public data.
173:§10 capped C++ at `Heuristic` and held Kotlin and Scala back from the fallback tier entirely, each for a real, language-specific ceiling: `tree-sitter-cpp`'s documented parsing ambiguity, `tree-sitter-kotlin`'s 61.2% structural-match ceiling against the real compiler AST, and `tree-sitter-scala`'s missing Scala 3 support. This section asks the follow-up question directly: is there a better answer than living with those ceilings, short of the full daemon/session-mode investment already deferred for `ra_ap_hir`? For all three languages, yes — a real middle tier exists, cheaper than a daemon and meaningfully better than tree-sitter, though it takes a different shape per ecosystem.
179:A pure-Rust alternative was checked for C specifically (C++ is a harder case, addressed below): `lang-c` (vickenty) claims "almost full C11" support, but was last _published_ to crates.io in September 2023 despite GitHub commits as recently as October 2025 — two years of merged-but- unreleased work, a real single-maintainer bus-factor signal. It's built on `rust-peg`, which has no built-in error-recovery machinery (an open, unshipped feature request, kevinmehall/rust-peg#276) — the same fail-fast risk that already ruled out `syn` for Rust in this spec (§3). No other credible pure-Rust C parser exists on crates.io. **Recommendation: don't build a bespoke native-Rust analyzer for C either** — ride it on the same optional Clang tier as C++, since C has none of C++'s ambiguity problem, making the incremental cost of covering it near zero once the C++ tier ex

